en a sequence $a_ i$, calculate how many different values $f$ takes on.\n\n-----Input-----\nThe input consists of two lines.\n - A single integer $1 \\leq n \\leq 5 \\cdot 10^5$, the length of the sequence.\n - The sequence of integers $a_1, a_2, \\dots , a_ n$. It is given that $1 \\leq a_ i \\leq 10^{18}$.\n\n-----Output-----\nOutput a single line containing a single integer, the number of distinct values $f$ takes on over the given sequence.\n\n-----Examples-----\nSample Input:\n4\n9 6 2 4\nSample Output:\n6", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, I need to solve this problem where I have to find the number of distinct GCD values of all possible subarrays in a given sequence. Let's think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, the problem statement says that for every possible subarray (i to j), compute the GCD of the elements from a_i to a_j and count how many unique GCDs there are. The challenge is to do this efficiently because the input size can be up to 5e5, which is 500,000 elements. A brute-force approach where we check every possible subarray and compute their GCDs would be O(n^2), which is way too slow for n=5e5. So I need a smarter way.\n\nHmm, I remember that there's a way to track the GCDs of subarrays ending at each position. Let me think. For each element in the array, we can track all possible GCD values of subarrays that end at that position. Then, the total unique GCDs across all these positions would be the answer.\n\nFor example, let's take the sample input: 9,6,2,4.\n\nFor the first element (9), the only subarray is [9], GCD is 9.\n\nFor the second element (6), the subarrays ending here are [6] and [9,6]. Their GCDs are 6 and GCD(9,6)=3. So for position 2, the GCDs are 6 and 3.\n\nFor the third element (2), the subarrays ending here are [2], [6,2], [9,6,2]. But instead of recomputing all, we can use the previous results. For each position i, we can keep track of a set (or a dictionary) of current GCDs. So when processing the third element, the possible GCDs are the GCD of each previous GCD with the current element, plus the current element itself. For the third element, the previous GCDs at position 2 are 6 and 3. So for each of those, compute GCD(gcd_val, current element). The current element is 2. So:\n\n- GCD(6,2) is 2.\n- GCD(3,2) is 1.\nPlus the new subarray [2] which is 2. So the set for position 3 would be {2,1}.\n\nWait, but adding the current element as a new subarray. So for each step, we start with the current element's value, then for each previous GCD in the previous set, compute the GCD with the current element and add those. Then, for each position, the set of GCDs is the union of the new GCDs computed this way. But since we are adding elements in a way that might merge duplicates (like if two different previous GCDs produce the same GCD when combined with current element), we can use a set or a dictionary to track unique values.\n\nSo the approach is:\n\nInitialize a set (maybe a list of unique values) that starts empty.\n\nFor each element in the array:\n\n- Take all GCD values from the previous element's set and compute GCD with the current element. Then add the current element's value itself.\n- Create a new set for the current element that contains all these computed GCDs. But since each step, when you compute the GCDs, some may be duplicates, so we can use a dictionary or a temporary set to track unique values.\n- Add all these new GCDs to a global set that keeps track of all unique GCDs encountered.\n\nWait, the algorithm could be structured as follows:\n\nWe maintain a list of current GCDs for the previous position. For each new element a[i], the current GCDs can be generated by taking each GCD in the previous list, compute the GCD of that with a[i], and then add a[i] itself. Then, we deduplicate these values to form the new list for the current position. Also, for each new GCD value in this current list, we add it to a global set that tracks all unique GCDs.\n\nFor example, in the sample input:\n\nProcessing first element (9):\n\nCurrent list is [9]. Add 9 to the global set. Global set becomes {9}.\n\nProcessing second element (6):\n\nTake each GCD in previous list (9) and compute GCD(9,6)=3. Then add the current element 6. So new list is [3,6]. Add these to the global set. Global set now has {9,3,6}.\n\nProcessing third element (2):\n\nPrevious list is [3,6]. Compute GCD(3,2)=1, GCD(6,2)=2. Add current element 2. New list is [1,2,2] but after deduplication, it's {1,2}. So new list is [1,2]. Add these to the global set (now {9,3,6,1,2}).\n\nProcessing fourth element (4):\n\nPrevious list is [1,2]. Compute GCD(1,4)=1, GCD(2,4)=2. Add current element 4. New list is [1,2,4]. Deduplicated, they are all unique. Add 1,2,4 to the global set. Wait, but 1 and 2 were already there. So the global set becomes {9,3,6,1,2,4}, which has 6 elements. Hence the sample output is 6.\n\nSo this approach works.\n\nThe key is that for each element, we only keep track of the GCDs of subarrays that end at the current position, and for each step, the number of these GCDs is small. Because when you compute the GCDs, they can only decrease or stay the same as the elements are processed. So each time you compute the GCDs for the next element, the number of possible GCDs is limited. For example, for a sequence of numbers, each step's current list can't have more than log2(a_i) elements, since each step the GCDs are factors of the previous GCDs and the new element. So this approach is O(n log a_i), which should be feasible for n up to 5e5.\n\nSo the steps are:\n\nInitialize a global set (to track all unique GCDs), and a previous list (to track the GCDs of subarrays ending at the previous element).\n\nFor each element in the array:\n\n1. Create a temporary set (or list) to hold the current GCDs.\n2. For each GCD value in the previous list, compute the GCD of that value with the current element. Add this to the temporary set.\n3. Also add the current element itself to the temporary set.\n4. Deduplicate the temporary set (convert to a set, then to a list, or use a data structure that automatically handles duplicates) to get the current list.\n5. Add all elements in the current list to the global set.\n6. Set the previous list to the current list for the next iteration.\n\nAt the end, the size of the global set is the answer.\n\nNow, implementing this in Python.\n\nBut wait, for each element, the previous list can be a list of unique GCDs, and for each new element, we compute the new GCDs. Let's see:\n\nIn Python, for each step, the previous list is a list of unique GCDs. For the new element a_i:\n\ncurrent_gcds = set()\n\nfor g in previous_gcds:\n    current_gcds.add(gcd(g, a_i))\n\ncurrent_gcds.add(a_i)\n\nBut this is a set, so duplicates are handled. Then, the new previous list is the elements of current_gcds. Then, we add all these elements to the global set.\n\nWait, but in code, for each step, we can compute the new set by taking the previous list, compute the GCDs, then add the current a_i. But using a set for current_gcds each time. Then, the previous list for the next step is the current_gcds (as a list or set). And each time, all elements of current_gcds are added to the global set.\n\nBut in Python, using sets for each step can be manageable, but for efficiency, perhaps using a list and checking for duplicates as we build it might be better. But given that for each step, the number of GCDs is small (like O(log a_i)), even with n=5e5, the total operations are acceptable.\n\nSo the code outline would be:\n\nRead n.\n\nRead the list of a_i.\n\nInitialize a global set.\n\nInitialize previous list as empty.\n\nFor each number in a_i:\n\n    current_set = set()\n    current_set.add(number)\n    for g in previous:\n        current_set.add(gcd(g, number))\n    # Now, current_set contains all possible GCDs for subarrays ending at current number\n    # Add all elements of current_set to the global set\n    global_set.update(current_set)\n    # Update previous to be the list of current_set for next iteration\n    previous = list(current_set)\n\nAt the end, the length of global_set is the answer.\n\nYes, that should work.\n\nNow, let's test this logic against the sample input.\n\nSample input:\n\n4\n\n9 6 2 4\n\nProcessing 9:\n\ncurrent_set = {9}\n\nglobal_set becomes {9}\n\nprevious = [9]\n\nProcessing 6:\n\ncurrent_set starts with 6. Then for each previous (9):\n\ngcd(9,6)=3. So current_set is {6,3}.\n\nglobal_set becomes {9,3,6}\n\nprevious = [3,6]\n\nProcessing 2:\n\ncurrent_set starts with 2. For previous elements 3 and 6:\n\ngcd(3,2)=1, gcd(6,2)=2. So current_set is {2,1}\n\nglobal_set adds 1 and 2. Now {1,2,3,6,9}\n\nprevious = [1,2]\n\nProcessing 4:\n\ncurrent_set starts with 4. Previous elements 1 and 2:\n\ngcd(1,4) =1, gcd(2,4)=2. So current_set is {4,1,2}.\n\nAdding these to the global_set: 4 is new. Now global_set is {1,2,3,4,6,9} \u2192 6 elements. Correct.\n\nSo the code works for the sample.\n\nAnother test case: n=1. The answer is 1.\n\nAnother test case: all 1s. Then every subarray's GCD is 1. So the answer is 1.\n\nAnother test case: 3 elements 2,4,8. Let's see:\n\nFirst element 2: current_set {2}, global {2}.\n\nSecond element 4: compute gcd(2,4)=2. current_set {4,2}. global adds 4 \u2192 {2,4}.\n\nThird element 8: compute gcd(2,8)=2; gcd(4,8)=4. current_set {8,2,4}. global adds 8. So total 3 elements. So answer is 3? Wait, what's the actual distinct GCDs.\n\nSubarrays:\n\n[2] \u21922.\n\n[2,4] \u21922.\n\n[4] \u21924.\n\n[2,4,8] \u21922.\n\n[4,8] \u21924.\n\n[8] \u21928.\n\nSo the distinct GCDs are 2,4,8. So answer is 3. Which matches.\n\nSo the code works here.\n\nSo the algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow, implementing this in Python.\n\nBut wait, the problem is with handling the previous set. For each element, we need to track all possible GCDs of subarrays ending at that element.\n\nIn Python, for each element, the previous list is a list of the GCDs from the previous step. Then, for each 