 of their neighbors. The solution involves analyzing consecutive frames and grouping them into runs of the same value, then counting these groups to determine the minimum number of frames.\n\n### Approach\nThe key insight is that each group of consecutive frames with the same value can be reduced to a single frame. This is because any frame in such a group (except the first and last) can be removed step-by-step until only one frame remains. The optimal solution is thus to count the number of such groups, as each group contributes exactly one frame to the minimum possible result.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    ptr = 0\n    T = int(input[ptr])\n    ptr += 1\n    for _ in range(T):\n        N = int(input[ptr])\n        ptr += 1\n        A = list(map(int, input[ptr:ptr+N]))\n        ptr += N\n        if N == 0:\n            print(0)\n            continue\n        count = 1\n        for i in range(1, N):\n            if A[i] != A[i-1]:\n                count += 1\n        print(count)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input:** The input is read in one go for efficiency, especially given the constraints.\n2. **Processing Each Test Case:** For each test case, we read the number of frames and their values.\n3. **Counting Groups:** We iterate through the list of frame values, counting how many times the value changes from one frame to the next. Each change indicates a new group of consecutive frames.\n4. **Output the Result:** The count of these groups is the minimum number of frames Chef can achieve, which is printed for each test case.\n\nThis approach efficiently processes each test case in linear time, making it suitable for large input sizes as specified in the problem constraints.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "code", "source": "taco", "test_cases": "{\"inputs\": [\"4\\n1\\n5\\n2\\n1 1\\n3\\n1 2 3\\n4\\n2 1 2 2\\n\"], \"outputs\": [\"1\\n1