considering previous flips), increment the flip count as this indicates a necessary prefix flip.\n2. **Single Flips Count**: Count the total number of 'B's in the string, as this represents the maximum number of single flips required if no prefix flips are used.\n3. **B Groups Calculation**: Count the number of contiguous groups of 'B's. Each group might require a separate prefix flip.\n4. **Merge Opportunity Check**: Check if there exists an 'A' between two 'B's. If such a position exists, merging two B groups into one can reduce the number of required prefix flips.\n\nThe minimum number of mutations is determined by considering the minimum of the initial prefix flips, the single flips count, and the B groups count if a merge opportunity exists.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(input[0])\n    s = input[1].strip()\n    \n    current_flips = 0\n    for i in reversed(range(n)):\n        current_char = s[i]\n        if current_flips % 2 == 1:\n            current_char = 'A' if current_char == 'B' else 'B'\n        if current_char == 'B':\n            current_flips += 1\n    initial_pf = current_flips\n    \n    bc = s.count('B')\n    \n    bg = 0\n    prev = ''\n    for c in s:\n        if c == 'B' and prev != 'B':\n            bg += 1\n        prev = c\n    \n    merge_opp = False\n    for i in range(1, n - 1):\n        if s[i] == 'A' and s[i-1] == 'B' and s[i+1] == 'B':\n            merge_opp = True\n            break\n    \n    ans = min(initial_pf, bc)\n    if merge_opp:\n        ans = min(ans, bg)\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Initial Prefix Flips Calculation**: By iterating from right to left, we determine how many prefix flips are needed. Each flip affects all characters to the left, so we track the number of flips to determine the current state of each character.\n2. **Single Flips Count**: Simply counting the 'B's gives the maximum number of single mutations required.\n3. **B Groups Calculation**: Groups of contiguous 'B's are counted, as each group might require a separate prefix flip.\n4. **Merge Opportunity Check**: If an 'A' is found between two 'B's, flipping this 'A' to 'B' merges two groups into one, potentially reducing the number of required flips.\n\nThe solution efficiently combines these checks to determine the minimum mutations needed, ensuring optimal performance even for large input sizes.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "code", "source": "taco", "test_cases": "{\"inputs\": [\"4\\nABBA\\n\", \"5\\nBBABB\\n\", \"12\\nAAABBBAAABBB\\n\"], \"outputs\": [\"2\\n\", \"2\\n\", \"4\\n\"]}", "starter_code": ""}, {"problem": "The Head Chef has opened N new restaurants across the city. But he is exhausted since he has to travel so much when he has to go from one restaurant to another. Further, the city traffic is driving him crazy. So he wishes to construct fly-overs between restaurants. A fly-over is a road at a raised height between any two restaurants. If two restaurants are connected by a fly-over, it takes only 1 second for the Chef to move from one restaurant to the other. When commuting between restaurants, our Head Chef wishes to use one or more fly-overs. He doesn't want to travel by the regular roads at all.\n\nUnfortunately, construction of fly-overs costs money. A construction company has agreed to build 1 fly-over for a payment of C rupees. It is up to the Chef to decide which restaurants he wants to connect using fly-overs. He asks his assistant chefs to give him suggestions. Each assistant chef proposes his own model. The Chef is now confused, he has no way of comparing models. Some models have too many fly-overs, while other models have too few.\n\nThe Sous Chef came up with a formula to estimate the Cost of a model. Suppose the model has F fly-overs. Let's label restaurants by numbers 1, 2, ..., N. Let T(U, V) represents the time taken to go from t