lem, we need to find the maximum possible GCD of the differences between the original array and a \"good\" sequence B, where the sum of B is exactly M. The key insight is that the GCD must be a divisor of the sum of the differences, which is fixed once M is given. By efficiently checking all possible divisors of this sum, we can determine the maximum GCD.\n\n### Approach\n1. **Calculate the Sum of Differences (K)**: For each test case, compute the sum of the differences K, which is the total sum of the array elements minus M.\n2. **Generate Divisors**: Find all divisors of K, sorted in descending order.\n3. **Check Validity of Divisors**: For each divisor, check if it is possible to form the sequence such that the GCD of the differences is the divisor. This is done by ensuring the sum of the integer divisions of each element by the divisor is sufficient to meet the required sum.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\nimport sys\nimport math\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    ptr = 0\n    T = int(input[ptr])\n    ptr += 1\n    for _ in range(T):\n        N, M = int(input[ptr]), int(input[ptr+1])\n        ptr +=2\n        A = list(map(int, input[ptr:ptr+N]))\n        ptr +=N\n        sum_A = sum(A)\n        K = sum_A - M\n        \n        def get_divisors(k):\n            divisors = set()\n            for i in range(1, int(math.isqrt(k)) +1):\n                if k % i == 0:\n                    divisors.add(i)\n                    divisors.add(k // i)\n            return sorted(divisors, reverse=True)\n        \n        divisors = get_divisors(K)\n        found = False\n        for d in divisors:\n            required = K // d\n            total = 0\n            for a in A:\n                total += a // d\n                if total >= required:\n                    break\n            if total >= required:\n                print(d)\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            print(1)  # This case should theoretically not occur since 1 is a divisor\n    \nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Reading Input**: The input is read in one go for efficiency, especially given the constraints.\n2. **Sum Calculation**: The sum of the array elements and the value K are computed.\n3. **Divisor Generation**: All divisors of K are generated using trial division up to the square root of K for efficiency.\n4. **Divisor Check**: For each divisor, check if it can be the GCD by ensuring the sum of the integer divisions of each element by the divisor meets the required threshold. The first valid divisor encountered (starting from the largest) is the answer.\n\nThis approach efficiently narrows down potential divisors and checks their validity, ensuring the solution is both optimal and efficient.", "ground_truth_solution": null, "domain": "code", "source": "taco", "test_cases": "{\"inputs\": [\"4\\n4 4\\n1 3 5 7\\n4 4\\n5 5 5 5\\n4 0\\n4 6 9 12\\n6 10\\n15 9 3 8 14 17\"], \"outputs\": [\"3\\n4\\n1\\n7\\n\"]}", "starter_code": ""}, {"problem": "Chef has fallen in love with Cheffina, and wants to buy N gifts for her. On reaching the gift shop, Chef got to know the following two things:\nThe cost of each gift is 1 coin.\nOn the purchase of every 4^{th} gift, Chef gets the 5^{th} gift free of cost. \n\nWhat is the minimum number of coins that Chef will require in order to come out of the shop carrying N gifts? \n\n------ Input Format ------ \n\n- The first line of input will contain an integer T \u2014 the number of test cases. The description of T test cases follows.\n- The first and only line of each test case contains an integer N, the number of gifts in the shop. \n\n------ Output Format ------ \n\nFor each test case, output on a new line the minimum number of coins that Chef will require to obtain all N gifts.\n\n------ Constraints ------ \n\n$1 \u2264 T \u2264 1000$\n$1 \u2264 N \u2264 10^{9}$\n\n----- Sample Input 1 ------ \n2\n5\n4\n----- Sample Output